WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.183 --> 00:00:01.140
(intriguing music)

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:03.570
- [Narrator] 362 million people

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:05.850
don't have internet access in China.

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:07.830
That's roughly 30 million more people

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:10.230
than the entire US population.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:12.960
But this rocket holds a possible solution

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.733
to help China close the gap.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:18.270
Inside it are low-Earth orbit satellites

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:20.640
capable of providing
high-speed internet access

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.860
even in remote areas.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:26.093
In China, this technology
is still experimental

00:00:26.093 --> 00:00:27.420
- [Controller] Ignition.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:28.380
- [Narrator] But Starlink,

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.900
a subdivision of the
American company SpaceX,

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.380
has already launched 4,768 of them,

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.600
making it the leader across the globe.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.210
Although space is vast,
these satellites operate

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:42.360
in a specific area that's
quickly getting more crowded.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.840
To ensure its place, China is
building up its capabilities.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.160
Just two decades ago,

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:50.340
59.1 million people in China were online,

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.250
just 4.6% of its population then.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:57.750
Now, it's over 1 billion
people, 74% of the population.

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:00.630
So is the country known
for its rapid development,

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:02.553
capable of rivaling Starlink?

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:06.990
Of the roughly 8,000
functioning satellites

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:11.310
currently in orbit, about
55% belong to Starlink

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:14.070
and the position of its satellites is key.

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:16.260
For decades, satellite internet providers

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.990
relied on geostationary satellites.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:20.880
These satellites are far away,

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.420
about 22,000 miles above
the Earth's surface.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.970
That distance lets them cover a large area

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:29.130
and reach remote parts of the globe,

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:31.020
but it takes longer for data to travel

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.000
between the satellite and the user.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.780
This means the broadband has
high latency and slower speeds.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.570
Often, geostationary
satellites offer speeds

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:41.610
at least 25 megabits per second,

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:43.500
which is enough to stream in HD

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.830
and can support about
one to three devices.

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:51.000
But satellites in low-Earth
orbit or LEO, are much closer,

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.390
no more than 1,200 miles
from Earth's surface.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:56.370
This means that while a LEO satellite

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:59.790
can't cover as much area as
a geostationary satellite,

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:02.943
it can provide more stable
and faster internet speeds.

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:07.740
Though SpaceX got its
start launching rockets,

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:10.200
it has built up a big
business selling broadband

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:11.763
powered by LEO satellites.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:14.400
And a key to its success?

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.103
It owns every step of the process.

00:02:17.103 --> 00:02:18.930
SpaceX manufactures the satellites

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:21.930
and launches them with its
own Falcon 9 is rockets

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:23.310
and Falcon 9 boosters,

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:26.760
the part that blasts the rocket
off the ground, are reusable

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:29.100
saving SpaceX money on launch costs.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:32.700
- They have a very capable launch division

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:37.700
that is regularly blasting
satellites off for customers,

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:42.150
for governments, and for itself.

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:43.830
- [Narrator] And it can
launch dozens of satellites

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:46.110
into low-Earth orbit per trip.

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:48.810
In 2014, China opened its space sector

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:50.490
up to private investment.

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.500
Five years later, startups in China

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.220
received over $314 million in funding.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:59.010
The only country that
attracted more startup funding

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:00.600
was the US.
- Ignition.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:01.560
- [Narrator] In the same year,

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:04.833
SpaceX alone received $962 million.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.530
To be more self-sufficient,

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:11.580
China has been investing in
key tech like LEO satellites.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:15.540
In 2021, it established
SatNet, a state-owned company

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:18.120
it hopes will streamline the
process to launch satellites

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.430
and form a broadband network.

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:24.483
So far, China says it will
build at least 7,800 satellites.

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:28.710
Plus, the China Aerospace Science
and Technology Corporation

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:31.290
and the China Aerospace Science
and Industry Corporation

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:33.510
have each launched experimental satellites

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:34.713
into low-Earth orbit.

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:37.260
And startup GalaxySpace

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:39.870
launched several operational
satellites last year

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:42.900
to begin its 1,000-satellite
constellation,

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:46.320
but so far, no enterprise in
China has created a rocket

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:47.760
to launch these satellites

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.670
that's as advanced as the Falcon 9.

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:53.220
However, Beijing Tianbing Technology

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:55.140
appears to be getting closer.

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:58.110
While the company doesn't
have a reusable rocket yet,

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:00.390
it sent a liquid fuel rocket into orbit

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:02.700
for the first time earlier this year.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:04.800
Experts say this is a crucial step

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.930
to creating a reusable rocket.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:09.390
Ultimately, the company
wants to develop rockets

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:12.840
that can deploy up to 60
satellites in a single launch,

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:15.030
similar to Falcon 9's capabilities.

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:18.720
- It's not easy to like get
off of Earth and like deploy,

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:20.670
you know, hundreds or thousands

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:23.760
or tens of thousands of satellites
into that part of space.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:26.160
You need a rocket that can,

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:29.880
you know, make those trips
over and over and over again.

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:32.760
Satellites in low-Earth
orbit have a shelf life,

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:35.700
they have to be replaced, so
you need to have a capability

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:39.750
to kind of continually launch satellites

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:41.040
- [Narrator] Altogether, this makes

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:43.200
the barrier to entry quite high.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.060
McKinsey estimates the cost to launch

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:49.140
an operable constellation
is between $5 to $10 billion

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:50.820
with yearly operating costs

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:52.683
running another $1 to $2 billion.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.500
So what's the benefit?

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:57.720
- The marketplace that SpaceX

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:00.705
has always been after is global.

00:05:00.705 --> 00:05:02.070
- [Narrator] SpaceX said in April

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:05.160
that Starlink was available
in more than 50 countries,

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:07.770
and within the next few
years, it expects to operate

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:10.470
in many more countries
across Africa and Asia.

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:13.830
- SpaceX has been going around the world

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:17.010
to get permission from national regulators

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:19.110
to sell Starlink connections.

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:20.400
- [Narrator] But it's not alone.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.380
Cooperation in space is a key part

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:24.300
of China's Belt and Road Initiative,

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:26.133
a global infrastructure project.

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:28.800
Last year, China said it had formed

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:31.410
149 space cooperation agreements

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:34.110
with 46 national space agencies

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:36.840
and already it's provided
countries across Africa,

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:38.850
Europe, and Asia with Beidou,

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:40.860
a global navigation satellite system

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:43.500
that rivals US-owned GPS.

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:44.790
Major Chinese companies

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:47.370
built infrastructure across the world too.

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:51.240
Up to 70% of 4G network
infrastructure across Africa

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:52.680
was built by Huawei.

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:56.640
- There are, you know, billions
of people around the world

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:58.650
that don't have have any internet at all.

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:01.470
Beyond that, there's like
huge demand just generally

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:04.620
for high-speed internet.

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:05.490
- [Narrator] And that demand

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:07.800
is part of what's driving Starlink, China

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:08.940
and companies everywhere

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:12.210
to build these LEO constellations, but-

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:14.370
- Satellite internet, at least right now,

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:17.310
faces like real cost challenges relative

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:21.960
to traditional on the ground
terrestrial infrastructure

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:23.580
for internet connections.

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:26.580
I don't think that like
low-Earth orbit satellites

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:29.280
will take over and provide

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:32.940
like global high-speed internet capacity.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:34.260
- [Narrator] One of the biggest concerns

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:35.700
facing the sector's expansion

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:38.190
is overcrowding in low-Earth orbit.

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:40.500
By 2030, US officials estimate

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:42.780
there will be 58,000 satellites,

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:47.610
about a 725% increase from the
number in the sky right now.

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:50.910
In just the past two years,
the FCC received applications

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.580
for more than 64,000 satellites,

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:55.890
and experts at NASA and the FCC

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:59.340
fear that more satellites
could mean more issues.

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:02.760
Already there are over
26,000 pieces of debris,

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:05.310
at least the size of a
softball in low-Earth orbit

00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:07.650
that could destroy a satellite on impact.

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:10.350
- If you assume that
like tens of thousands

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:12.720
of additional satellites
are launched and deployed

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:15.480
to low-Earth orbit in the coming years,

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:19.020
there's gonna be a real need
for coordination communication,

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:22.020
finding ways for all these operators

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:27.020
to sort of know where
their devices are located,

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:28.560
how they're moving and what

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:31.923
like the potential threat
is for a collision.

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:36.390
Low-Earth orbit, it's sort
of like a commons right now.

00:07:36.390 --> 00:07:40.140
There's some treaties that
govern space and there are also

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:42.750
what experts and government
officials call norms

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:45.180
around good space behavior.

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:47.610
It's a little more
open-ended and dependent

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:52.610
on actors there to sort of
work together and communicate.

00:07:52.655 --> 00:07:55.572
(intriguing music)

